00:11:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Ah, brilliant question! You've just zeroed in on the precise definition that makes this whole technology possible. You're absolutely right—chemically, water vapor is H₂O, identical to liquid water. So, what does "waterproof" actually mean in this context? It comes down to the **phase of matter** and the physical behavior of molecules in each phase, not their chemical identity. In a **liquid**, the H₂O molecules are bonded together in a cohesive, flowing mass (droplets) due to surface tension. They act as a team. To block this liquid, a barrier only needs to be solid enough and have holes small enough that the cohesive droplet can't squeeze through. In a **gas (vapor)**, the H₂O molecules are flying solo, completely independent of one another. There is no surface tension binding them. They bounce around individually with much higher energy. So, the jacket isn't a magical "H₂O filter" that sorts molecules by type. It's a **mechanical phase-filter**. Think of it like this: - **The Jacket's Pores:** A huge crowd of people linked arm-in-arm (liquid water) cannot pass through a single doorway. But if they unlink and go through one at a time (water vapor), they can pass through the same doorway easily. The doorway didn't change; the physical state of the people did. The claim is **"liquid-water-proof,"** not "impermeable to all H₂O molecules in any state under all conditions." If a jacket were truly impermeable to individual vapor molecules, it would be a vapor barrier (like a rubber glove), and you'd be drenched in your own sweat instantly. That's not the goal. The goal is to keep rain (liquid) out while letting sweat (vapor) escape. Technically, materials like rubber *are* "waterproof" in the absolute sense, blocking *everything*. But the outdoor industry defined a new functional category: **waterproof/breathable**, meaning it resists liquid water penetration (often up to a specific pressure measured in a hydrostatic head test) while allowing 